        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_016Gq2qg8WDPeaw7iruLcSwP",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path decisions both enforce centralized configuration and access control through a single resolver at startup.\\ntimestamp: 2025-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Dependency-Injection\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both pages explicitly describe a single authoritative resolver at startup; the pattern they share is directly stated in each.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2026-01-14\\n---\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\nCredential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are not separate concerns \u2014 they are two applications of the same architectural principle: **all mutable, security-sensitive, or environment-dependent configuration must be resolved in exactly one place at startup, before any downstream code has a chance to discover or override it on its own.**\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] ensures that the Anthropic API key enters the system once, at the moment the child process is spawned, and never lives in the Hub\u0027s own environment. This prevents accidental or malicious leakage through process inspection or inheritance.\\n\\n[[runtime-paths]] ensures that every file-system location \u2014 wiki roots, data directories, secrets storage, agent instruction paths \u2014 is resolved once at startup by a single authoritative resolver. This prevents scattered, ad-hoc path discovery downstream and ensures all runtime decisions flow from the same validated source.\\n\\n## Why They Belong Together\\n\\nBoth decisions answer the same question: *Where does this critical piece of configuration come from, and who decides it?* By forcing the answer through a single resolver (credentials injected at spawn; paths read and validated at startup), the system achieves two things:\\n\\n1. **Auditability**: every sensitive choice has a single source of truth, making security review and troubleshooting tractable.\\n2. **Consistency**: downstream code cannot discover its own paths or credentials \u2014 it must use what the resolver provides, eliminating subtle inconsistencies and privilege escalation through unguarded configuration discovery.\\n\\nThe pattern applies equally to transient credentials (spawned once per process) and persistent file locations (resolved once per Hub startup), because both need the same protection: centralized, validated composition before anything downstream runs.\\n\u0022}"
        },
