      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are two applications of the same underlying architectural principle: **centralized composition at a single point, before any downstream code runs.**\n\n**Credential Scoping** [[credential-scoping]] ensures the Anthropic API key is injected into each spawned agent\u0027s environment at the moment of process creation, never inherited from or stored in the Hub\u0027s own environment. This prevents accidental or malicious leakage through process inspection.\n\n**Runtime Paths** [[runtime-paths]] ensures every file-system location the Hub touches \u2014 wiki roots, data directories, secrets storage, agent instruction paths \u2014 is resolved exactly once at startup by a single authoritative resolver, preventing scattered ad-hoc path discovery downstream.\n\nBoth decisions protect the system by making configuration discoverable only through one validated channel:\n\n1. **Auditability**: every security-sensitive choice has a single source of truth, so review and troubleshooting are tractable.\n2. **Consistency**: downstream code cannot side-step the resolver to discover its own paths or credentials, eliminating subtle inconsistencies and privilege escalation risks.\n\nThe pattern applies equally to transient credentials (injected at spawn) and persistent file locations (resolved at startup) because both need the same protection: controlled, centralized composition instead of unguarded discovery.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], which synthesizes how these two decisions reinforce the same security and consistency goal.",
